const title = "Electric Scooter Accident Lawyer Calhoun County | Battle Creek MI Attorneys";
const description = "Injured in an electric scooter accident in Calhoun County? Our Battle Creek attorneys fight rental companies and negligent drivers for maximum compensation. Free consultation. 45+ years experience.";

const faqItems = [
  {
    question: "Do I need to wear a helmet when riding an electric scooter in Michigan?",
    answer: "Michigan law does not currently require helmets for electric scooter riders over 18. However, wearing one is strongly recommended as helmets significantly reduce the risk of traumatic brain injuries. Not wearing a helmet does not automatically prevent you from recovering compensation if injured, though insurance companies may argue comparative negligence."
  },
  {
    question: "Can I recover compensation if the scooter I rented was defective?",
    answer: "Yes. Rental companies have a duty to maintain their scooters in safe working condition and inspect them regularly. If mechanical failure caused your accident, you may have claims against both the rental company for negligent maintenance and potentially the manufacturer for product defects. Our attorneys investigate all potential sources of liability."
  },
  {
    question: "What if I was partially at fault for my scooter accident?",
    answer: "Michigan follows modified comparative negligence. You can still recover compensation if you were partially at fault, but your damages will be reduced by your percentage of fault—unless you were more than 50% responsible. Even if you made mistakes, the other party's negligence may still entitle you to significant compensation."
  },
  {
    question: "How long do I have to file a claim after a Calhoun County scooter accident?",
    answer: "The statute of limitations for personal injury claims in Michigan is generally three years from the date of injury. For no-fault insurance benefits, you must notify your insurer within one year. However, gathering evidence and building your case takes time. Contacting an attorney as soon as possible after your accident protects your rights and strengthens your claim."
  },
  {
    question: "Can I sue if a pothole or road defect caused my scooter accident?",
    answer: "Potentially. Municipalities have a duty to maintain roads in reasonably safe condition. However, governmental immunity provides significant protection to cities and counties. Successfully pursuing these claims requires proving the municipality had notice of the dangerous condition and failed to act. Our attorneys understand how to navigate governmental immunity defenses."
  },
  {
    question: "What damages can I recover after an electric scooter accident?",
    answer: "Available compensation depends on the circumstances of your accident and may include medical expenses (past and future), lost wages and diminished earning capacity, pain and suffering, scarring and permanent disfigurement, physical therapy and rehabilitation costs, and adaptive equipment if you suffer permanent disability. We work with medical and economic experts to calculate the full value of your claim."
  }
];
