<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <ArticleHero />
  <ArticleContent>
    <p>Electric scooters line the streets of Battle Creek, Marshall, and communities throughout Calhoun County. These rentable scooters promise convenient transportation—but they come with serious risks. Riders with no training or experience can rent a scooter in seconds using a smartphone app. Many operate recklessly, racing through traffic to reach their destination quickly. When accidents happen, riders suffer broken bones, road rash, head trauma, and life-altering injuries.</p>

    <p>At Logeman &amp; Iafrate, P.C., our <a href="/service-area/calhoun-county-mi">Calhoun County electric scooter accident attorneys</a> have spent over 45 years fighting for injury victims throughout Michigan. We understand the unique legal challenges scooter accidents present—from determining liability among multiple parties to navigating Michigan's complex insurance laws. When you're facing mounting medical bills and an uncertain recovery, we're prepared to take on rental companies, negligent drivers, and municipalities to secure the compensation you deserve.</p>

    <h2>How Electric Scooter Accidents Happen in Calhoun County</h2>

    <p>Most scooter accidents occur when riders lose control, fall from the scooter, or collide with vehicles, cyclists, or pedestrians. Common causes include:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Potholes and pavement defects:</strong> Calhoun County roads contain cracks, uneven surfaces, and potholes that can throw riders from scooters traveling at speeds up to 15 mph.</li>
      <li><strong>Vehicle collisions:</strong> Drivers turning right often fail to check for scooters in bike lanes or alongside traffic. Left-turning vehicles strike scooters crossing intersections.</li>
      <li><strong>Mechanical failures:</strong> Defective brakes, worn tires, and malfunctioning steering mechanisms cause riders to lose control without warning.</li>
      <li><strong>Inadequate maintenance:</strong> Rental companies frequently fail to inspect and repair scooters, leaving dangerous units in circulation.</li>
      <li><strong>Rider inexperience:</strong> Most scooter operators have no training before their first ride and make critical errors in traffic.</li>
      <li><strong>Intoxication:</strong> Riders using scooters after drinking lack the reaction time and judgment necessary to operate safely.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>These accidents often result in catastrophic injuries. Riders thrown from scooters at speed suffer traumatic brain injuries, spinal cord damage, broken bones, and severe road rash requiring skin grafts. Unlike motorcyclists, scooter riders rarely wear protective gear. Fewer than 10% wear helmets, leaving them vulnerable to devastating head injuries.</p>

    <h2>Who Is Liable for Your Calhoun County Scooter Accident?</h2>

    <p>Determining liability in electric scooter cases requires thorough investigation. Multiple parties may share responsibility for your injuries:</p>

    <h3>The Rental Company</h3>
    <p>Bird, Lime, Spin, and other rental companies owe riders a duty to provide safe, properly maintained equipment. When they fail to inspect scooters, ignore maintenance needs, or leave defective units available for rent, they can be held liable for resulting injuries. Rental companies may also face liability for inadequate user instructions and safety warnings.</p>

    <h3>Negligent Drivers</h3>
    <p>When a driver strikes a scooter rider, standard motor vehicle negligence principles apply. Drivers who fail to yield, make unsafe turns, or operate while distracted can be held accountable through third-party liability claims. These claims allow recovery for pain and suffering, excess medical expenses, and full wage loss—damages not available through no-fault benefits alone.</p>

    <h3>Municipalities</h3>
    <p>Cities and counties throughout Calhoun County have a duty to maintain roadways in reasonably safe condition. When dangerous road defects—unmarked construction zones, missing warning signs, or hazardous pavement conditions—cause scooter accidents, municipalities may face premises liability claims.</p>

    <h3>Manufacturers</h3>
    <p>Defective scooter design, faulty batteries, inadequate braking systems, and manufacturing defects can lead to product liability claims against scooter manufacturers and component suppliers.</p>

    <h2>Compensation Available After a Calhoun County Scooter Accident</h2>

    <p>Michigan law provides multiple avenues for recovery after electric scooter accidents:</p>

    <h3>No-Fault Insurance Benefits</h3>
    <p>If a motor vehicle strikes you while riding a scooter, you're entitled to Personal Injury Protection (PIP) benefits through Michigan's no-fault system. These benefits cover medical expenses, wage loss (up to 85% of gross income), and replacement services—regardless of who caused the accident. Understanding your coverage level before an accident is critical, as policy limits vary from $50,000 to unlimited.</p>

    <h3>Third-Party Liability Claims</h3>
    <p>When your injuries meet Michigan's serious impairment threshold, you can pursue compensation from at-fault drivers for pain and suffering, excess economic damages, and losses beyond PIP coverage. Our attorneys have extensive experience proving threshold injuries and maximizing third-party recoveries.</p>

    <h3>Premises Liability and Product Liability Claims</h3>
    <p>Claims against rental companies, municipalities, or manufacturers proceed under traditional negligence and product liability principles, allowing recovery for medical expenses, lost income, pain and suffering, scarring and disfigurement, and permanent disability.</p>

    <h2>Michigan's Scooter Regulations and Your Rights</h2>

    <p>Under Michigan law, motorized scooters may not be operated in bike lanes but can be ridden on roads in the lane closest to the right curb. Local ordinances often prohibit scooter use on sidewalks. However, these regulations remain inconsistently enforced, creating confusion and danger for riders and pedestrians alike.</p>

    <p>Violating these regulations doesn't automatically bar your right to compensation. Even if you were riding on a sidewalk when struck by a vehicle, you may still recover damages if the driver was negligent. Our attorneys understand how to navigate comparative fault arguments and protect your recovery.</p>

    <h2>Why You Need an Experienced Scooter Accident Attorney</h2>

    <p>Electric scooter accident cases present unique challenges that require specialized knowledge and aggressive advocacy:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Multiple defendants:</strong> Cases often involve rental companies with sophisticated legal teams, insurance carriers fighting to deny claims, and municipalities with governmental immunity defenses.</li>
      <li><strong>Complex liability analysis:</strong> Determining fault requires accident reconstruction, mechanical inspection, and expert testimony about industry standards.</li>
      <li><strong>Disputed injuries:</strong> Insurance companies routinely argue that scooter injuries were pre-existing or stem from unrelated causes.</li>
      <li><strong>Novel legal theories:</strong> Scooter litigation continues to evolve as courts address new questions about rental company duties and municipal liability.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>At Logeman &amp; Iafrate, we don't back down from these challenges. We work with accident reconstructionists, mechanical engineers, and medical experts to build compelling cases. We've spent decades taking on insurance companies and corporations that deny legitimate claims—and we've built a track record of results that speaks for itself.</p>

    <h2>Protecting Your Rights After a Calhoun County Scooter Accident</h2>

    <p>The actions you take immediately after a scooter accident can significantly impact your ability to recover compensation:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Seek medical attention immediately, even if injuries seem minor. Symptoms of traumatic brain injury often don't appear until hours or days after impact.</li>
      <li>Report the accident to local police and obtain a copy of the accident report.</li>
      <li>Document the scene with photos showing the scooter's condition, road defects, and your visible injuries.</li>
      <li>Preserve evidence by taking screenshots of your rental transaction and any safety warnings provided by the app.</li>
      <li>Gather witness contact information from anyone who saw the accident occur.</li>
      <li>Report the accident to the rental company through their app, but avoid giving detailed statements without legal counsel.</li>
      <li>Contact an experienced scooter accident attorney before speaking with insurance adjusters.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Don't let insurance companies pressure you into quick settlements that don't account for long-term medical needs. Rental companies and their insurers want to minimize payouts—they're not on your side. You need fierce advocates who understand the full value of your claim and are prepared to fight for every dollar you deserve.</p>

    <h2>Contact Our Calhoun County Electric Scooter Accident Attorneys</h2>

    <p>If you've been injured in an electric scooter accident in Battle Creek, Marshall, or anywhere in Calhoun County, don't wait to protect your rights. The attorneys at Logeman &amp; Iafrate, P.C. offer free consultations and work on a contingency-fee basis—you pay nothing unless we win your case.</p>

    <p>Call us at <a href="tel:+17349940200"><strong>(734) 994-0200</strong></a> or fill out our online contact form to schedule your free consultation. Let us handle the legal fight while you focus on healing. We take cases others won't, and we win cases others can't.</p>

